  "content": {
    "title": "Veteran coach not surprised by success of Arizona Wildcats basketball",
    "text": "April 4, 2026Updated April 5, 2026, 7:43 a.m. MTINDIANAPOLIS — Gonzaga coach Mark Few feels like a proud father. Arizona coach Tommy Lloyd was on his staff for two decades before taking the head coaching gig in Tucson five years ago.Now Lloyd is thriving, with a team in the NCAA Final Four, and fresh off a contract extension. His record in his five years is an impressive 148-36, including the Final Four loss to Michigan on April 4, and this year's Wildcats boast 14 wins over nationally ranked teams — the most by any program in a season.Few was in town for the Naismith Basketball Hall of Fame news conference at Lucas Oil Stadium on April 4, held a few hours before Lloyd's Wildcats tipped off against Michigan.Few has been in town for a few days, and he had a chance to catch up with Lloyd earlier in the week.And no, he is not surprised at Lloyd's success.\"Well, apple doesn't fall far from the tree,\" he said. \"We had 20 awesome years together. Our families grew up together. He started on my staff as basically a volunteer and kind of a grad aide. He was just a relentless worker with a really positive spirit. He developed a great feel for the game.\"As he progressed, there wasn't any doubt that he was going to be a great head coach. When he took the Arizona thing, I thought it was just a great move on their part to see that as an assistant, and I'm just so happy for him.\"Few said his advice to Lloyd early on was to pick one thing he could focus on and develop that aspect. For Lloyd, that was developing the international pipeline. He did that for Few and continued it in Tucson, with the current roster boasting two foreign-born starters in Ivan Kharchenkov and Motiejus Krivas.Few said the happiness for his former protege kicked in last week when the Wildcats punched their ticket to Indianapolis with a 79-64 win over Purdue in the Elite Eight in San Jose.\"When they got over that game last weekend, the joy in our family was just incredible and feelings of pride,\" he said.He also isn't surprised Lloyd rejected the chance to go to North Carolina.\"He's happy there,\" Few said. \"You can't get better than being happy where you're at.\"",
